<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petén</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Petén Basin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Petén is the northern lowland region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, largely covered by tropical forest and now forming the country's largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. In the Classic period it was the heartland of Maya civilisation, with cities such as Tikal, Naranjo, Waka' and Uaxactun. The lakes around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-peten-itza">Lake Petén Itzá</a> later sheltered the Itza kingdom of Nojpetén, the last independent Maya state, until the Spanish conquest of 1697.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peten.jpg" alt="Petén" width="320" height="280" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simon Burchell · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peten_Map_v3_May_archaeological_sites.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
